RangeForce: Lateral Movement with Metasploit
Platform: RangeForce
Room: Lateral Movement with Metasploit
Difficulty: Advanced
Date: 2026
Objective
The objective of this lab was to perform lateral movement within a compromised network using Metasploit. The focus was on gaining initial access via reverse shells, conducting enumeration, extracting credential data, and pivoting to additional systems.
Step 1: Establishing Initial Access
I began by gaining initial access to the target system using a reverse shell.
During this process, I:
Established a connection to the compromised host
Confirmed remote command execution capabilities
This highlighted how initial access serves as the foundation for further exploitation.
Step 2: System Enumeration
Next, I performed enumeration on the compromised system to gather information about the environment.
This allowed:
Identification of system details and network configuration
Discovery of additional targets within the network
This demonstrated the importance of enumeration in identifying opportunities for lateral movement.
Step 3: Dumping Credential Hashes
I then extracted credential data from the compromised system.
This involved:
Dumping password hashes from the system
Identifying valid user accounts
This step showed how credential access can enable further compromise across the network.
Step 4: Pivoting to Other Systems
Using the gathered information and credentials, I performed pivoting to access additional machines.
This allowed:
Routing traffic through the compromised host
Expanding access to other systems within the network
This demonstrated how attackers can move laterally after gaining an initial foothold.
Step 5: Lateral Movement with Metasploit
I utilized Metasploit modules to facilitate lateral movement across the network.
This resulted in:
Successful access to additional systems
Expansion of control within the environment
This confirmed how frameworks like Metasploit can streamline multi-stage attacks.
Step 6: Impact Analysis
Combining all findings, the environment was vulnerable due to several factors:
Initial system compromise via reverse shell
Insufficient monitoring of internal activity
Exposure of credential hashes
Lack of network segmentation
An attacker could leverage these issues to:
Move laterally across multiple systems
Compromise additional hosts
Escalate access within the network
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this lab, I gained hands-on experience performing lateral movement using Metasploit. This exercise reinforced the importance of strong credential protection, network segmentation, and monitoring of internal traffic.
It also highlighted how attackers can chain multiple techniques—initial access, enumeration, credential dumping, and pivoting—to fully compromise a network.
